{
  "dom_id": "step-q107819",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?",
  "worked_solution": "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$",
